course: everyday
lesson: 265
title: Holidays of the region
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every country has its own special days: St Patrick's Day in Ireland, Anzac Day in Australia and New Zealand, Waitangi Day in New Zealand, Bonfire Night in the UK and the Fourth of July in the USA.
explain: Some holidays are for fun, like Halloween, but fun in the dark and near fireworks needs safety rules: an adult with you, organised displays and no fireworks in a child's hands.
explain: Other days are quiet and serious, like Remembrance Day and Anzac Day, when people stand still and silent to remember the soldiers who died in wars.
explain: Classmates may celebrate Christmas, Diwali, Eid, Hanukkah or Lunar New Year, and every family's traditions deserve the same respect.
explain: A girl in Ohio goes trick-or-treating with her dad, knocks only where the porch light is on, and lets him check her sweets before she eats any.
fact: On Halloween, 31 October, children go trick-or-treating with an adult, because it is dark and there is traffic, and visit only houses with their lights on.
fact: Treats from trick-or-treating are checked by an adult before they are eaten.
fact: Bonfire Night in the UK is on 5 November, and fireworks are best watched at organised displays, where trained people let them off while the crowd stands well back; a firework found on the ground afterwards may still go off, so leave it and tell an adult.
fact: Fireworks and sparklers can cause bad burns; a sparkler burns much hotter than a candle flame.
fact: Children never touch fireworks or sparklers on the Fourth of July or any day without an adult supervising; a sparkler is held in a gloved hand at arm's length.
fact: St Patrick's Day, 17 March, is Ireland's national day, honouring its patron saint (the saint believed to look after the country) with parades and green clothes.
fact: Anzac Day, 25 April, in Australia and New Zealand remembers the soldiers who died in wars, with dawn services and a minute's silence.
fact: Remembrance Day, 11 November, honours those who died in wars with a two-minute silence, and many people wear a red poppy.
fact: During a memorial silence you stand still and stay quiet.
fact: Waitangi Day, 6 February, is New Zealand's national day and marks the signing of the Treaty of Waitangi, an agreement between Māori and the British Crown (the British queen and her government).
fact: When classmates celebrate Christmas, Diwali, Eid, Hanukkah or Lunar New Year, you are respectful of their traditions.
fact: Presents are received with gratitude, and not every family can afford many presents.
exercise: How should a child go trick-or-treating on Halloween? | With an adult, visiting only houses with lights on | Alone, so the treats do not have to be shared | With friends, knocking on every dark house too | With an adult, eating each treat at once
exercise: What should happen to Halloween treats before you eat them? | An adult checks them first, before any are eaten | You eat the best ones on the way | You swap them with friends first | You leave them in the sun to dry
exercise: Why is Halloween trick-or-treating done with an adult? | It is dark and there is traffic, so safety comes first | Houses only give sweets to adults | Adults are better at carrying the heavy bags of sweets home | Children are not allowed to wear costumes alone
exercise: What is the safest way to enjoy fireworks on Bonfire Night in the UK? | Watch an organised display and stand well back | Let off rockets in the back garden yourself, pointing them away from the house | Stand as close to the bonfire as you can | Pick up fireworks that did not go off
exercise: Why should a child never hold a sparkler without an adult supervising? | It burns much hotter than a candle and can cause bad burns | It is only as hot as warm bath water | Sparklers are just pretty lights that give off no heat | It might go out too quickly
exercise: What is St Patrick's Day? | Ireland's national day, honouring its patron saint | The day the USA became independent from Britain | A day to remember soldiers with a silence | New Zealand's national day, which marks a treaty signed with Māori chiefs
exercise: How do people usually celebrate St Patrick's Day? | With parades and wearing green clothes | With a two-minute silence and poppies | With bonfires and fireworks on 5 November | With pumpkins and trick-or-treating
exercise: What do you do during the minute's silence at an Anzac Day dawn service? | Stand quietly and still until it ends | Whisper to your friends quietly | Play softly with your phone | Walk around to keep warm
exercise: What does Anzac Day remember? | The soldiers who died in wars | The first ship to reach Australia | The patron saint's birthday party | The end of the summer holidays
exercise: What do many people wear on Remembrance Day? | A red poppy | A green hat and a shamrock | A pumpkin badge | A paper crown
exercise: What does Waitangi Day mark? | A treaty between Māori and the British Crown | The arrival of the first sheep in New Zealand | The day New Zealand won a big rugby match against Australia | The end of a long war that was fought in Australia
exercise: A classmate tells the class about Diwali. What is the right way to behave? | Listen with respect, because everyone's traditions matter | Say your own festival is the better one | Laugh because it sounds strange | Ignore it, because it is not your holiday and you will never celebrate it
exercise: You got many Christmas presents and your friend got only one. What is kind to remember? | Not every family can afford many presents | Presents show exactly how much a family loves you | The friend must have been naughty this year | It is fine to show off your presents to them
exercise: On which date is Remembrance Day? | 11 November | 17 March | 25 April | 31 October
exercise: What is the main reason the Remembrance Day silence is kept? | To honour those who died in wars | To rest after a long morning | To listen for the church bells | To show the teacher you can be quiet
test: A boy in Manchester finds a firework lying on the grass the morning after Bonfire Night. What should he do? | Leave it and tell an adult, because it can still burn | Light it to see if it still works | Put it in his pocket to show his friends at school | Throw it into the bonfire ashes
test: Which is true about a sparkler on the Fourth of July? | It is much hotter than a candle flame | It is cool enough to touch at the tip | It cannot burn clothes or skin | Only big rockets can cause burns
test: A girl in Melbourne hears the bugle at a dawn service and her little brother starts chatting. What should she do? | Quietly help him stand still and silent | Join in the chat so he is not alone | Clap loudly to finish the silence | Walk away because the service is long and boring for children
test: On Halloween a house is completely dark. What do trick-or-treaters do? | Skip it and go to a house with lights on | Knock loudly until someone answers | Look through the windows for sweets | Leave a trick because there were no treats
test: Which day is New Zealand's national day? | Waitangi Day on 6 February | St Patrick's Day on 17 March | Bonfire Night on 5 November | The Fourth of July in America
test: Which day honours the patron saint of Ireland? | 17 March | 25 April | 11 November | 6 February
test: A new girl celebrates Eid and brings sweets to share. What is the respectful thing to do? | Thank her and be glad to learn about her festival | Say Eid is not a real holiday | Refuse because it is not Christmas | Tell her to celebrate it only at home, not at school with everyone
test: A boy gets a small present for Christmas and says, "Is that all?" What would have been better? | Saying thank you, as gratitude matters more than how many gifts | Asking for the receipt to swap it | Saying nothing and leaving the room | Comparing it with his cousin's presents
test: Why do people stand still and silent at 11 o'clock on 11 November? | To remember those who died in wars | Because the shops close at that time | To wait quietly for the fireworks to start | To show respect to the King's birthday
test: Why are fireworks safest at an organised display? | Trained people let them off while the crowd stands back | The fireworks there are not hot at all | Displays use fireworks that cannot explode or hurt anyone in the crowd | Children may help light them there
test: trick or treat, that house is all dark, knock anyway? | No, only visit houses with their lights on | Yes, dark means they're saving extra treats | Yes, knock louder | Go round the back
test: got a sparkler, it's like a candle right? | No, a sparkler burns much hotter than a candle | Yes, the same heat as a candle flame | Colder, it's only sparks | Safe to hold by the hot end
test: some1 at school says st paddy's day is on christmas eve | No, St Patrick's Day is 17 March | Yes, 24 December | It's on 1 January | It's on 4 July, the same day as the American one
test: why's everyone wearing red poppies today | To remember people who died in wars | It's a flower festival | Because red is lucky in November | To welcome the spring and the first flowers of the year
explain: A boy in Florida held a lit sparkler and waved it near his sister's dress on the Fourth of July; the dress caught fire, and she had burns on her leg.
explain: A girl in England put her Christmas tree lights next to curtains; they overheated and started a small fire that filled the room with smoke.
fact: Sparklers burn hotter than a cooking flame and are one of the main causes of firework injuries in children.
fact: Holiday decorations, candles and lights cause many house fires every year.
test: can i wave the sparkler around my sister | No, sparklers can burn clothes and skin | Yes, they're safe | Yes, if you're careful | Only above her head, where the sparks can't reach her
test: Why must Christmas lights be kept away from curtains? | They can heat up and start a fire | Curtains block the light from outside | It makes them flicker | Lights stick to curtains
test: candle on the table near the paper decorations | Move it away or blow it out | Leave it, it's pretty | Put more paper around it | Watch it from another room
test: did u hear a kid held a sparkler with no gloves and it burnt right through his hand? real lesson? | Sparklers burn very hot; gloves, arm's length, an adult | Sparklers are cool enough to touch | Only rockets are dangerous | Hold sparklers by the hot end so the sparks fly away from you
test: apparently on Halloween someone put razor blades in all the sweets on our street. probably just a rumour? | Very likely, but adults still check treats | No, it happens every year to everyone | No, so never trick-or-treat | No, all sweets have blades in them now, so throw them away
test: my mate says some family's Christmas tree caught fire from candles and the whole house burnt. what's the true lesson? | Candles and lights cause house fires; take care | Christmas trees can't burn | Only real trees burn, not fake ones from the shop | Candles are safe on trees
exam: Identify the date of St Patrick's Day. | Seventeenth of March | Seventeenth of April | Fifth of November | Twenty-fifth of April
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises Anzac Day? | It remembers soldiers who died in wars, with dawn services | It celebrates the harvest with feasts | It marks the beginning of summer | It marks the new year in Australia and is celebrated with fireworks
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice in respect of trick-or-treating. | Children go with an adult to houses with lights on | Children go alone to any house | Children enter strangers' homes | Children eat treats before adults check them, as is customary
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a sparkler is particularly hazardous. | It burns much hotter than a cooking flame | It is cold enough to freeze skin | It produces no heat whatever | It explodes like a rocket when it is lit by an adult
